## 📂 Dotfiles GitOps Architecture

A declarative, event-driven GitOps workflow for managing configuration files across mixed Windows and Linux environments. This setup isolates host environments using custom machine branches, protects the core baseline from direct modifications, and triggers background synchronization using hardware states (battery/power events via udev and systemd).

Internal configurations are managed cleanly using GNU Stow to safely map files to local home directories without pollution.
------------------------------

## 📐 1. Branching Strategy

To keep machine configurations entirely decoupled while preserving a shared foundation, the repository relies on a hub-and-spoke branching model:

          [ Protected main Branch ]  <--- Core shared updates, alias profiles, core configs
                     │
         ┌───────────┴───────────┐
         ▼                       ▼
[ host-linux-laptop ]   [ host-win-desktop ] <--- Machine-isolated branches
  (udev profiles,          (PowerShell hooks,
   systemd sync)            registry modifications)

## main Branch (Protected Sandbox)

* Purpose: Holds the absolute minimum baseline configuration shared by all systems (e.g., universal shell aliases, agnostic .vimrc, editor themes).
* Rule: Directly pushing or force-pushing to main from a client environment is completely blocked by automated Git hooks. Upstream modifications must happen via Pull Requests (PRs).

## Host Branches (host-<os>-<identifier>)

* Purpose: Tracks system-specific anomalies (e.g., custom screen resolutions, machine-specific paths, environment variables).
* Rule: Each hardware environment checks out its own designated host branch. Background synchronizations commit and push exclusively to these isolated channels, ensuring merge conflicts are physically impossible.

